  '[{"question_text":"Which of the following is true about String memory management in Java when using the new keyword?","options":["It creates an object only in the String Constant Pool.","It creates a copy in the stack memory directly.","It always creates a new object in the regular heap area.","It does not allocate new memory if the string exists."],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A and D describe standard string literal behavior. B is incorrect because objects are never stored directly on the execution stack.","A and D describe standard string literal behavior. B is incorrect because objects are never stored directly on the execution stack.","The new keyword guarantees creation of a new object in the heap memory outside the pool.","A and D describe standard string literal behavior. B is incorrect because objects are never stored directly on the execution stack."]},{"question_text":"Consider the following code snippet. What will be the output?\n\n```java\npublic class Test {\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        Integer a = 127;\r\n        Integer b = 127;\r\n        Integer c = 128;\r\n        Integer d = 128;\r\n        System.out.println((a == b) + \" \" + (c == d));\r\n    }\r\n}\n```","options":["true true","false false","true false","false true"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A, B, and D fail to account for the internal JVM optimization cache limits.","A, B, and D fail to account for the internal JVM optimization cache limits.","Java caches Integer objects from -128 to 127. Values outside this range (like 128) yield separate object allocations.","A, B, and D fail to account for the internal JVM optimization cache limits."]},{"question_text":"What happens to the return value if a primitive variable is modified inside a finally block after a try block returns it?","options":["The value changed in the finally block is returned.","The original value from the try block is returned.","The program terminates immediately with an error.","The method returns null instead of the primitive value."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A is true only if the finally block contains its own explicit return statement. C and D are false behaviors.","For primitive types, the return value is evaluated and saved before the finally block executes.","A is true only if the finally block contains its own explicit return statement. C and D are false behaviors.","A is true only if the finally block contains its own explicit return statement. C and D are false behaviors."]},{"question_text":"Given the following class definitions, what happens when you attempt to compile this code?\n\n```java\nclass Parent {\r\n    public void display() throws java.io.IOException {}\r\n}\r\nclass Child extends Parent {\r\n    @Override\r\n    public void display() throws Exception {}\r\n}\n```","options":["It compiles without warnings or errors.","Compilation fails due to the exception type.","Compilation fails because of the override annotation.","It compiles but throws a runtime exception."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A and D are incorrect since it''s a compile-time rule violation. C is a valid annotation usage here.","An overriding subclass method cannot declare a broader checked exception (Exception) than its superclass method.","A and D are incorrect since it''s a compile-time rule violation. C is a valid annotation usage here.","A and D are incorrect since it''s a compile-time rule violation. C is a valid annotation usage here."]},{"question_text":"What is the fundamental performance difference between HashMap and TreeMap?","options":["HashMap is $O(n)$ for search, while TreeMap is $O(1)$.","HashMap is $O(1)$ for search, while TreeMap is $O(\\log n)$.","HashMap is $O(\\log n)$ for search, while TreeMap is $O(1)$.","Both have identical $O(1)$ search performance properties."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D misrepresent the logarithmic and hash-table lookup properties of these collection types.","HashMap uses direct bucket hashing ($O(1)$ average), while TreeMap uses a balanced Red-Black tree structure ($O(\\log n)$).","A, C, and D misrepresent the logarithmic and hash-table lookup properties of these collection types.","A, C, and D misrepresent the logarithmic and hash-table lookup properties of these collection types."]},{"question_text":"Consider the following code involving polymorphism. What will be the output?\n\n```java\nclass A {\r\n    int x = 10;\r\n    void print() { System.out.println(\"A\"); }\r\n}\r\nclass B extends A {\r\n    int x = 20;\r\n    void print() { System.out.println(\"B\"); }\r\n}\r\npublic class Test {\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        A obj = new B();\r\n        System.out.print(obj.x + \" \");\r\n        obj.print();\r\n    }\r\n}\n```","options":["10 A","20 B","10 B","20 A"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A, B, and D ignore the core rule that data fields do not exhibit dynamic runtime polymorphism in Java.","A, B, and D ignore the core rule that data fields do not exhibit dynamic runtime polymorphism in Java.","Instance variables are resolved by the reference type (A), while methods are resolved by the runtime object type (B).","A, B, and D ignore the core rule that data fields do not exhibit dynamic runtime polymorphism in Java."]},{"question_text":"Which of the following accurately describes a NullPointerException?","options":["It is a checked exception caught at compile time.","It is an unchecked exception extending RuntimeException.","It occurs when an array goes past its boundaries.","It occurs when dividing an integer value by zero."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A is false. C describes an index error. D describes an arithmetic exception.","It is an unmonitored runtime error thrown when trying to perform operations on an unassigned reference pointer.","A is false. C describes an index error. D describes an arithmetic exception.","A is false. C describes an index error. D describes an arithmetic exception."]},{"question_text":"What happens if a concrete class implements an interface but leaves some abstract methods unimplemented?","options":["The class compiles but cannot be instantiated.","The compiler rejects it unless declared abstract.","The missing methods get default empty bodies.","The application crashes strictly at runtime execution."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D are incorrect because Java enforces interface completeness at the compilation level.","A class must either fully realize all inherited interface blueprints or be marked explicitly with the abstract modifier.","A, C, and D are incorrect because Java enforces interface completeness at the compilation level.","A, C, and D are incorrect because Java enforces interface completeness at the compilation level."]},{"question_text":"Which statement is correct about the finalize() method in Java?","options":["It is always guaranteed to execute immediately.","It has been deprecated in modern Java versions.","It is called manually to free native stack memory.","It forces the immediate execution of garbage collection."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A is false because GC timing is non-deterministic. C and D mistake it for manual memory management tools.","finalize() is deprecated due to unpredictable timing, performance overhead, and potential resource deadlocks.","A is false because GC timing is non-deterministic. C and D mistake it for manual memory management tools.","A is false because GC timing is non-deterministic. C and D mistake it for manual memory management tools."]},{"question_text":"How does encapsulation protect internal object state?","options":["By declaring all methods inside the class static.","By restricting data access via private class modifiers.","By compiling classes into dynamic library fields.","By making all instance fields public and accessible."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D break or ignore standard object-oriented scoping definitions.","Encapsulation hides state fields using access restrictions and routes all external access through safe public accessor methods.","A, C, and D break or ignore standard object-oriented scoping definitions.","A, C, and D break or ignore standard object-oriented scoping definitions."]},{"question_text":"What will be the output of the following code snippet?\n\n```java\npublic class Main {\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        int x = 5;\r\n        System.out.println(x++ + ++x);\r\n    }\r\n}\n```","options":["10","11","12","13"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A, B, and D stem from misapplying unary operation sequencing rules.","A, B, and D stem from misapplying unary operation sequencing rules.","x++ evaluates to 5 (x becomes 6). Then ++x changes x to 7 and evaluates to 7. Result: $5 + 7 = 12$.","A, B, and D stem from misapplying unary operation sequencing rules."]},{"question_text":"Which of the following statements is true regarding an abstract class in Java?","options":["It must contain at least one abstract method.","It can be instantiated directly using the new keyword.","It can have concrete methods, variables, and constructors.","It is completely blocked from implementing interfaces."],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A is false (can be empty). B is false (cannot be instantiated). D is false (can implement interfaces).","A is false (can be empty). B is false (cannot be instantiated). D is false (can implement interfaces).","Abstract classes can contain complete business logic, structural constructors, and fields along with abstract declarations.","A is false (can be empty). B is false (cannot be instantiated). D is false (can implement interfaces)."]},{"question_text":"What happens when a code block throws a checked exception but does not catch it or declare it?","options":["The program compiles normally but fails at runtime.","The Java compiler flags it as a compilation error.","The JVM automatically wraps it in a RuntimeException.","The exception is silently ignored during execution."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D are invalid because the source file will not compile into bytecode.","Java enforces static safety via the \"catch or declare\" requirement for all checked exception pathways.","A, C, and D are invalid because the source file will not compile into bytecode.","A, C, and D are invalid because the source file will not compile into bytecode."]},{"question_text":"What is the default value of an uninitialized instance variable of reference type in Java?","options":["0","empty","null","undefined"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A is for primitives. B and D are not valid language initialization literals.","A is for primitives. B and D are not valid language initialization literals.","Object handles defined at class-level scopes default automatically to reference the null pointer state.","A is for primitives. B and D are not valid language initialization literals."]},{"question_text":"Consider the following code. What will be the output?\n\n```java\nclass Base {\r\n    public static void display() { System.out.println(\"Base\"); }\r\n}\r\nclass Derived extends Base {\r\n    public static void display() { System.out.println(\"Derived\"); }\r\n}\r\npublic class Main {\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        Base obj = new Derived();\r\n        obj.display();\r\n    }\r\n}\n```","options":["Base","Derived","Compile-time error","RuntimeException"],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["Class methods (static) are bounded statically at compilation based on reference type, demonstrating method hiding instead of overriding.","B assumes dynamic dispatch rules apply. C and D are incorrect since hiding is standard legal behavior.","B assumes dynamic dispatch rules apply. C and D are incorrect since hiding is standard legal behavior.","B assumes dynamic dispatch rules apply. C and D are incorrect since hiding is standard legal behavior."]},{"question_text":"Which of the following blocks is always executed regardless of whether an exception is thrown or caught?","options":["catch","try","finally","throw"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A requires an error match. B houses the execution paths. D acts as an active signal emitter.","A requires an error match. B houses the execution paths. D acts as an active signal emitter.","The finally block guarantees cleanup block execution under routine flow control states.","A requires an error match. B houses the execution paths. D acts as an active signal emitter."]},{"question_text":"How does Java''s Pass-by-Value mechanism behave when passing an object reference to a method?","options":["It copies the entire underlying object data.","It copies the address pointing to the object.","It changes directly to pass-by-reference mode.","It locks the object into a read-only state."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A would consume too much memory. C is historically inaccurate for Java. D is structurally false.","Variables store references, not literal objects. Passing copies this pointer handle value into the method argument.","A would consume too much memory. C is historically inaccurate for Java. D is structurally false.","A would consume too much memory. C is historically inaccurate for Java. D is structurally false."]},{"question_text":"Which OOP concept is primarily achieved by using private fields along with public getters and setters?","options":["Inheritance","Polymorphism","Encapsulation","Abstraction"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A structures trees. B handles polymorphic behavior variation. D models general high-level operational workflows.","A structures trees. B handles polymorphic behavior variation. D models general high-level operational workflows.","Isolating instance properties from raw external interaction secures safe internal state manipulation.","A structures trees. B handles polymorphic behavior variation. D models general high-level operational workflows."]},{"question_text":"What is the main structural difference between final, finally, and finalize?","options":["All three are identical access control modifiers.","final is a modifier, finally a block, finalize a method.","They are interchangeable keywords in exception handling.","final controls threads, while the others control classes."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D misrepresent basic grammatical classifications in the language syntax.","The terms occupy completely distinct spaces across structural visibility, exception processing, and system lifecycle management.","A, C, and D misrepresent basic grammatical classifications in the language syntax.","A, C, and D misrepresent basic grammatical classifications in the language syntax."]},{"question_text":"Which of the following conditions will cause a StackOverflowError?","options":["Creating too many large arrays in the heap.","Infinite or highly deep recursive method calls.","Attempting to cast an object to an invalid subclass.","Accessing an array location outside its bounds."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A leads to heap depletion. C is a casting issue. D represents bounds constraint errors.","Every active invocation appends frame information onto execution memory stacks; unstopped loops exhaust this tracking cache.","A leads to heap depletion. C is a casting issue. D represents bounds constraint errors.","A leads to heap depletion. C is a casting issue. D represents bounds constraint errors."]},{"question_text":"What is the difference between == and .equals() when comparing two String objects?","options":["Both check reference memory locations identically.","== checks value equality, .equals() checks memory.","== checks memory addresses, .equals() checks text values.",".equals() is only available for primitive value types."],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A and B swap or confuse standard operator behavior definitions. D is false since primitives cannot call methods.","A and B swap or confuse standard operator behavior definitions. D is false since primitives cannot call methods.","== is an identity operator tracking address parity, whereas .equals() is overridden in String to compare literal character sequences.","A and B swap or confuse standard operator behavior definitions. D is false since primitives cannot call methods."]},{"question_text":"What happens if a constructor is declared private inside a class?","options":["The class cannot have any static methods inside it.","The class cannot be instantiated from outside its scope.","The class must be marked with the abstract keyword.","It triggers a compile-time error automatically."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A and C represent unrelated structural rules. D is false as private constructors are completely legal.","Private constructors prevent external allocation, a strategy widely adopted in singleton and utility class configurations.","A and C represent unrelated structural rules. D is false as private constructors are completely legal.","A and C represent unrelated structural rules. D is false as private constructors are completely legal."]},{"question_text":"What is the default layout format of memory storage inside a standard Java array?","options":["Linked nodes dispersed randomly across the heap space.","Contiguous block allocations of memory references or data.","Dynamic hashing tables that grow and resize on demand.","Stack memory zones that clean themselves up automatically."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A describes lists. C describes map sets. D describes localized execution thread memory areas.","Arrays in Java allocate solid sequential index blocks in heap storage for uniform element lookups.","A describes lists. C describes map sets. D describes localized execution thread memory areas.","A describes lists. C describes map sets. D describes localized execution thread memory areas."]},{"question_text":"What will be the output of the following code snippet?\n\n```java\npublic class Test {\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        System.out.println(10 + 20 + \"Java\" + 10 + 20);\r\n    }\r\n}\n```","options":["30Java30","1020Java1020","30Java1020","Java301020"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A, B, and D fail to correctly track the evaluation path and type conversion changes.","A, B, and D fail to correctly track the evaluation path and type conversion changes.","Operations run left to right. $10 + 20$ adds up to 30. Meeting the String switches operations to text concatenation: \"30Java\" + 10 + 20 -> \"30Java1020\".","A, B, and D fail to correctly track the evaluation path and type conversion changes."]},{"question_text":"Which interface must a class implement to allow its instances to be sorted using Collections.sort()?","options":["Serializable","Cloneable","Comparable","Comparator"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A handles saving streams. B duplicates instances. D is passed as an external sorting controller strategy.","A handles saving streams. B duplicates instances. D is passed as an external sorting controller strategy.","Comparable specifies natural structural ordering via the compareTo implementation directly within target object architectures.","A handles saving streams. B duplicates instances. D is passed as an external sorting controller strategy."]},{"question_text":"Which type of polymorphism is demonstrated by method overloading in Java?","options":["Runtime Polymorphism","Compile-time Polymorphism","Dynamic Method Binding","Structural Inheritance"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A and C describe virtual dispatch at runtime. D is an unrelated architectural description.","Method overloading is resolved during compilation based strictly on parameter signatures, often called static binding.","A and C describe virtual dispatch at runtime. D is an unrelated architectural description.","A and C describe virtual dispatch at runtime. D is an unrelated architectural description."]},{"question_text":"What is the effect of applying the final keyword to a method definition?","options":["The method cannot be overloaded in the same class.","The method cannot be overridden by any subclass.","The method can only access static class properties.","The method is hidden from external class consumers."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A is incorrect since overloading works fine. C describes static constraints. D describes private visibility behavior.","final methods prevent derived classes from changing their internal implementation logic.","A is incorrect since overloading works fine. C describes static constraints. D describes private visibility behavior.","A is incorrect since overloading works fine. C describes static constraints. D describes private visibility behavior."]},{"question_text":"Which of the following exceptions is considered an unchecked exception?","options":["java.io.IOException","java.sql.SQLException","java.lang.ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException","java.lang.ClassNotFoundException"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A, B, and D extend Exception directly, making them checked conditions that require explicit validation handling.","A, B, and D extend Exception directly, making them checked conditions that require explicit validation handling.","Index out of bounds errors derive from RuntimeException, classifying them as unchecked behaviors.","A, B, and D extend Exception directly, making them checked conditions that require explicit validation handling."]},{"question_text":"Can a class implement two interfaces that declare default methods with the identical name and signature?","options":["Yes, without any extra code or adjustments required.","No, it causes an immediate unresolvable compiler failure.","Yes, if the class overrides the conflicting method explicitly.","No, unless one interface is converted into an abstract class."],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A creates ambiguity issues. B is false because overriding resolves it. D is an invalid structural requirement.","A creates ambiguity issues. B is false because overriding resolves it. D is an invalid structural requirement.","Java demands the implementing class override the method to resolve the multiple inheritance naming ambiguity manually.","A creates ambiguity issues. B is false because overriding resolves it. D is an invalid structural requirement."]},{"question_text":"What will be the output of the following code snippet?\n\n```java\npublic class ScopeTest {\r\n    static int x = 11;\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        int x = 22;\r\n        System.out.println(ScopeTest.x);\r\n    }\r\n}\n```","options":["11","22","0","Compile error"],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["Using the class prefix ScopeTest.x explicitly targets the class field, bypassing the localized scope variable variable x.","B is what local printing would yield. C is the uninitialized static default. D is wrong because shadowing is completely legal.","B is what local printing would yield. C is the uninitialized static default. D is wrong because shadowing is completely legal.","B is what local printing would yield. C is the uninitialized static default. D is wrong because shadowing is completely legal."]},{"question_text":"What is the primary operational rule for an instance initializer block in a class?","options":["It runs only when the class is loaded by the JVM.","It runs every time a new instance is created, before constructors.","It runs immediately after the constructor logic finishes.","It must be manually executed using a system call."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A describes static initializer blocks. C and D invert or misstate object lifecycle order rules.","Instance blocks execute during object construction steps, initializing fields just before the constructor body code runs.","A describes static initializer blocks. C and D invert or misstate object lifecycle order rules.","A describes static initializer blocks. C and D invert or misstate object lifecycle order rules."]},{"question_text":"Which of the following is true about a static inner class?","options":["It holds a hidden reference to its outer class instance.","It can access instance fields of its outer class directly.","It can be instantiated without creating an outer class instance.","It cannot contain any static methods or variables."],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A and B describe non-static inner classes. D is incorrect since static members are fully permitted inside them.","A and B describe non-static inner classes. D is incorrect since static members are fully permitted inside them.","Static nested classes behave like top-level classes packed inside namespaces, needing no outer structural instance context.","A and B describe non-static inner classes. D is incorrect since static members are fully permitted inside them."]},{"question_text":"What is the behavior of the instanceof operator when evaluated against a null reference?","options":["It throws a NullPointerException at runtime.","It returns true for any Object type check.","It returns false without raising any exception.","It causes a compile-time structural type error."],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A, B, and D contradict Java''s built-in handling design rules for the safety operator.","A, B, and D contradict Java''s built-in handling design rules for the safety operator.","Java defines instanceof to return false if the target evaluation operand evaluates to a null state reference.","A, B, and D contradict Java''s built-in handling design rules for the safety operator."]},{"question_text":"What will happen during compilation and execution of this code snippet?\n\n```java\npublic class Main {\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        try {\r\n            System.out.print(\"Try \");\r\n            throw new RuntimeException();\r\n        } catch (Exception e) {\r\n            System.out.print(\"Catch \");\r\n        } finally {\r\n            System.out.print(\"Finally\");\r\n        }\r\n    }\r\n}\n```","options":["Try Catch","Try Finally","Try Catch Finally","Catch Finally"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A, B, and D skip active block sections that are executed under standard flow conditions.","A, B, and D skip active block sections that are executed under standard flow conditions.","The code enters try, hits the exception, jumps immediately into matching catch processing, and wraps up through the finally execution path.","A, B, and D skip active block sections that are executed under standard flow conditions."]},{"question_text":"Which design technique achieves runtime polymorphism in Java?","options":["Method Overloading","Method Overriding","Variable Shadowing","Operator Overloading"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A represents static type resolution. C alters visibility scoping. D is not a native user-facing feature in Java.","Method overriding lets subclasses re-engineer base class functions, with invocations routed dynamically at runtime based on the actual object type.","A represents static type resolution. C alters visibility scoping. D is not a native user-facing feature in Java.","A represents static type resolution. C alters visibility scoping. D is not a native user-facing feature in Java."]},{"question_text":"What memory management action happens when an object becomes unreachable?","options":["It is instantly wiped out of heap memory spaces.","It is marked by the JVM and cleaned during a future GC sweep.","It gets migrated safely over into the stack memory frame.","Nothing, unless the system calls System.gc() explicitly."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A is incorrect because execution is non-deterministic. C is invalid. D is false since GC triggers automatically under memory constraints.","Unreachable nodes are tracked as garbage candidates, and their memory is reclaimed asynchronously during GC cycles.","A is incorrect because execution is non-deterministic. C is invalid. D is false since GC triggers automatically under memory constraints.","A is incorrect because execution is non-deterministic. C is invalid. D is false since GC triggers automatically under memory constraints."]},{"question_text":"Which keyword allows a subclass constructor to invoke a parent class constructor?","options":["this","super","parent","extends"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A routes internal sibling constructor chaining. C is not a keyword. D defines inheritance structures on class headers.","The super() call links constructor execution up the inheritance chain and must be placed at the first line of the child constructor.","A routes internal sibling constructor chaining. C is not a keyword. D defines inheritance structures on class headers.","A routes internal sibling constructor chaining. C is not a keyword. D defines inheritance structures on class headers."]},{"question_text":"What is the consequence of catching a generic Exception before a specific NullPointerException?","options":["The application runs significantly slower.","The compiler flags it as unreachable code error.","The specific catch block acts as the fallback option.","The program compiles but errors out on startup."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D are incorrect because Java strictly enforces catch block ordering rules during compilation.","Since Exception matches everything, a downstream specialized sub-class block can never be reached, which triggers a compilation failure.","A, C, and D are incorrect because Java strictly enforces catch block ordering rules during compilation.","A, C, and D are incorrect because Java strictly enforces catch block ordering rules during compilation."]},{"question_text":"What will be the output of the following code snippet?\n\n```java\npublic class Test {\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        String s1 = \"hello\";\r\n        String s2 = new String(\"hello\");\r\n        System.out.println(s1 == s2.intern());\r\n    }\r\n}\n```","options":["true","false","NullPointerException","Compile error"],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["Calling .intern() fetches or places the string text into the pool, returning the matching pool reference address which equals s1.","B ignores the structural function of pool interning. C and D represent completely normal and legal operations here.","B ignores the structural function of pool interning. C and D represent completely normal and legal operations here.","B ignores the structural function of pool interning. C and D represent completely normal and legal operations here."]},{"question_text":"Which of the following fields can be defined within a standard Java interface in Java 7 and earlier?","options":["Protected instance fields","Private variable data metrics","Public static final constants","Volatile runtime dynamic properties"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A, B, and D violate visibility restrictions defined for legacy interface structural models.","A, B, and D violate visibility restrictions defined for legacy interface structural models.","Traditional interfaces allow only implicitly defined public static final fields alongside abstract method signatures.","A, B, and D violate visibility restrictions defined for legacy interface structural models."]},{"question_text":"What is the effect of casting a subclass object reference back to its parent class type?","options":["The underlying object type is altered permanently.","It restricts accessible members to those defined in the parent.","It throws a ClassCastException error at runtime.","It completely clones the existing object data properties."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A and D imply structural object modifications. C describes an error that occurs during invalid downcasting instead.","Upcasting shifts the reference perspective view to the parent layout scope, though the underlying object retains its original identity.","A and D imply structural object modifications. C describes an error that occurs during invalid downcasting instead.","A and D imply structural object modifications. C describes an error that occurs during invalid downcasting instead."]},{"question_text":"What will be the output of this execution block?\n\n```java\npublic class LogicalTest {\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        boolean a = false;\r\n        if (a = true) { System.out.println(\"True\"); }\r\n        else { System.out.println(\"False\"); }\r\n    }\r\n}\n```","options":["True","False","Compile-time type warning","No output is printed"],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["The single equality symbol = handles variable assignment. a = true assigns true to a and evaluates to true, satisfying the condition.","B assumes a comparison occurred. C and D are incorrect since assigning expressions in tests is valid.","B assumes a comparison occurred. C and D are incorrect since assigning expressions in tests is valid.","B assumes a comparison occurred. C and D are incorrect since assigning expressions in tests is valid."]},{"question_text":"Which keyword is applied to class specifications to block further inheritance extensions?","options":["static","final","abstract","private"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A handles scoping modifiers. C demands inheritance setup lines. D manages member visibility boundaries.","Setting a class definition header as final restricts downstream subclassing or architectural branch modifications.","A handles scoping modifiers. C demands inheritance setup lines. D manages member visibility boundaries.","A handles scoping modifiers. C demands inheritance setup lines. D manages member visibility boundaries."]},{"question_text":"What happens if an exception is thrown inside a catch block and there is no outer wrapper try structure?","options":["The application safely ignores it and moves on.","The execution flow halts, passing it up the call stack.","The finally block is skipped to prevent crashing.","The catch block loops back to its beginning state."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D violate basic exception propagation mechanics and execution safety designs.","Exceptions raised in catch processing abort standard logic, firing up the call stack frame layer looking for new handlers.","A, C, and D violate basic exception propagation mechanics and execution safety designs.","A, C, and D violate basic exception propagation mechanics and execution safety designs."]},{"question_text":"What structural constraint applies to method configurations when overriding a parent method?","options":["The visibility modifier must be made more restrictive.","The return type must be transformed to a primitive.","The visibility must be equal to or broader than the parent.","The method name must append the super prefix string."],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A breaks external polymorphism contracts. B and D introduce imaginary syntax rules.","A breaks external polymorphism contracts. B and D introduce imaginary syntax rules.","Subclasses cannot narrow the public footprint of an inherited method; access can only stay identical or open wider.","A breaks external polymorphism contracts. B and D introduce imaginary syntax rules."]},{"question_text":"What will be the output of the following code snippet?\n\n```java\npublic class DoubleTest {\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n  