   public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        System.out.println(5 / 2);\r\n    }\r\n}\n```","options":["2.5","2","3","2.0"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A and D assume automatic floating-point promotion. C represents an invalid mathematical rounding step.","Dividing two integers performs integer division, discarding the remainder and truncating the result toward zero.","A and D assume automatic floating-point promotion. C represents an invalid mathematical rounding step.","A and D assume automatic floating-point promotion. C represents an invalid mathematical rounding step."]},{"question_text":"Which structural configuration is required to declare an effective Singleton class design?","options":["Make all instance methods static inside the class scope.","Use a private constructor and a public static getter method.","Inherit from a dedicated structural Singleton abstract class.","Implement the modern functional interface marker system."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A describes a utility class, not an object pattern. C and D introduce invalid design patterns.","A private constructor prevents unauthorized allocation, and a static method controls access to a single managed instance.","A describes a utility class, not an object pattern. C and D introduce invalid design patterns.","A describes a utility class, not an object pattern. C and D introduce invalid design patterns."]},{"question_text":"What happens if a catch block declares an argument type that is completely unrelated to the thrown exception?","options":["The compiler errors out immediately due to type mismatches.","The block is skipped, and the exception continues up the stack.","The JVM forces a runtime cast correction step.","The exception is automatically suppressed by the block."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A is incorrect since catching unrelated exceptions is valid as long as they are thrown in the try block. C and D are false behaviors.","If an exception type does not match the catch block''s criteria, the JVM skips it and checks downstream or up the call stack.","A is incorrect since catching unrelated exceptions is valid as long as they are thrown in the try block. C and D are false behaviors.","A is incorrect since catching unrelated exceptions is valid as long as they are thrown in the try block. C and D are false behaviors."]},{"question_text":"What is the structural trait of an abstract method declaration?","options":["It must have private visibility access constraints.","It contains an empty braces body implementation block.","It ends with a semicolon instead of an execution block.","It must be defined inside a final class configuration."],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A and D violate OOP inheritance rules. B defines a concrete method with an empty implementation.","A and D violate OOP inheritance rules. B defines a concrete method with an empty implementation.","Abstract methods act as interface specifications; they have no body implementation and end directly with a semicolon.","A and D violate OOP inheritance rules. B defines a concrete method with an empty implementation."]},{"question_text":"What will be the output of the following code snippet?\n\n```java\npublic class BooleanTest {\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        System.out.println(null instanceof Object);\r\n    }\r\n}\n```","options":["true","false","Compile error","NullPointerException"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D contradict the language specification for instanceof operations involving null evaluations.","The instanceof operator is null-safe; checking a null reference against any type always returns false.","A, C, and D contradict the language specification for instanceof operations involving null evaluations.","A, C, and D contradict the language specification for instanceof operations involving null evaluations."]},{"question_text":"How do you invoke a specific overridden parent method from inside a subclass instance method?","options":["super.methodName()","this.methodName()","Parent.methodName()","super().methodName()"],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["The super prefix bypasses local subclass overrides to target the immediate parent class''s method implementation.","B loops locally. C works only for static methods. D uses constructor syntax invalid for standard method calls.","B loops locally. C works only for static methods. D uses constructor syntax invalid for standard method calls.","B loops locally. C works only for static methods. D uses constructor syntax invalid for standard method calls."]},{"question_text":"Which of the following is true regarding Checked exceptions in Java?","options":["They inherit directly from the Error class hierarchy.","They must be handled or declared at compile time.","They are thrown exclusively by the Java Virtual Machine.","They are bypassed during continuous integration testing."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A defines critical system issues. C is false since user code can throw them. D is an unrelated testing concept.","Java enforces safety checks for checked exceptions during compilation, requiring a try-catch block or a throws declaration.","A defines critical system issues. C is false since user code can throw them. D is an unrelated testing concept.","A defines critical system issues. C is false since user code can throw them. D is an unrelated testing concept."]},{"question_text":"What will be the output of the following code snippet?\n\n```java\npublic class EqualityTest {\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        StringBuilder sb1 = new StringBuilder(\"Hi\");\r\n        StringBuilder sb2 = new StringBuilder(\"Hi\");\r\n        System.out.println(sb1.equals(sb2));\r\n    }\r\n}\n```","options":["true","false","Compile error","Runtime Exception"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A assumes content evaluation like String. C and D are incorrect since calling equals on StringBuilder is valid.","StringBuilder does not override Object.equals(). It falls back to reference equality (==), which evaluates to false for separate heap objects.","A assumes content evaluation like String. C and D are incorrect since calling equals on StringBuilder is valid.","A assumes content evaluation like String. C and D are incorrect since calling equals on StringBuilder is valid."]},{"question_text":"Which OOP principle focuses on hiding internal implementation complexity by exposing a simplified interface?","options":["Inheritance","Polymorphism","Abstraction","Encapsulation"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A structures relationships. B enables multiple behavioral forms. D focuses on access restriction and data protection.","A structures relationships. B enables multiple behavioral forms. D focuses on access restriction and data protection.","Abstraction hides complex details behind simple interfaces or abstract classes, whereas encapsulation focuses on data security.","A structures relationships. B enables multiple behavioral forms. D focuses on access restriction and data protection."]},{"question_text":"What is the behavior of the try-with-resources statement?","options":["It accelerates memory allocation speeds for collections.","It automatically closes resources that implement AutoCloseable.","It prevents checked exceptions from being thrown entirely.","It re-routes execution tasks onto external backgrounds."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D misstate the purpose of this resource management architecture.","Introduced in Java 7, try-with-resources ensures that any resource declaring AutoCloseable is closed automatically when the block finishes.","A, C, and D misstate the purpose of this resource management architecture.","A, C, and D misstate the purpose of this resource management architecture."]},{"question_text":"What will be the output of the following code snippet?\n\n```java\npublic class StringPoolTest {\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        String s1 = \"ABC\";\r\n        String s2 = \"AB\" + \"C\";\r\n        System.out.println(s1 == s2);\r\n    }\r\n}\n```","options":["true","false","Compile-time error","Runtime Exception"],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["Constant string expressions (like \"AB\" + \"C\") are optimized at compile time into single literals, matching the existing \"ABC\" reference in the pool.","B assumes separate object allocations. C and D are wrong because this optimization is completely standard.","B assumes separate object allocations. C and D are wrong because this optimization is completely standard.","B assumes separate object allocations. C and D are wrong because this optimization is completely standard."]},{"question_text":"What restriction applies to overloading a method signature in Java?","options":["The return types must be completely different.","The visibility scopes must match the parent layout.","The parameter lists must differ in type, count, or order.","The method must declare a broader exceptions matrix."],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A, B, and D are incorrect because changing the return type or exception list alone is not enough to overload a method.","A, B, and D are incorrect because changing the return type or exception list alone is not enough to overload a method.","Method overloading relies on unique method signatures, which are defined by their parameter configurations.","A, B, and D are incorrect because changing the return type or exception list alone is not enough to overload a method."]},{"question_text":"Which exception is thrown when an application tries to load a class via its string name but cannot find it?","options":["java.lang.NullPointerException","java.lang.ClassNotFoundException","java.lang.NoClassDefFoundError","java.lang.IllegalArgumentException"],"correct_option_