tion rules; only the first matching block executes.","B, C, and D mistake catch selection rules; only the first matching block executes."]},{"question_text":"What is the effect of declaring an interface method as default?","options":["It makes the method private to its declaring interface.","It allows the interface to provide a concrete method body.","It blocks implementing classes from overriding that method.","It forces the method to run inside a separate background thread."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D run counter to why default method extensions were introduced.","Introduced in Java 8, default methods let interfaces add new functionality with concrete implementations without breaking existing concrete implementations.","A, C, and D run counter to why default method extensions were introduced.","A, C, and D run counter to why default method extensions were introduced."]},{"question_text":"Which of the following is true about garbage collection in Java?","options":["Developers can predict exactly when the GC will run.","Calling System.gc() forces the JVM to collect garbage immediately.","The GC reclaims memory allocated on the heap space.","The GC manages local stack frame memory allocations."],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A and B are incorrect because GC timing is non-deterministic. D is incorrect because stack memory cleans up automatically as frames pop.","A and B are incorrect because GC timing is non-deterministic. D is incorrect because stack memory cleans up automatically as frames pop.","The garbage collector manages heap memory by reclaiming space used by unreachable objects.","A and B are incorrect because GC timing is non-deterministic. D is incorrect because stack memory cleans up automatically as frames pop."]},{"question_text":"What will be the output of the following code snippet?\n\n```java\npublic class ConcatTest {\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        System.out.println(\"Result: \" + (5 * 2));\r\n    }\r\n}\n```","options":["Result: 52","Result: 10","Result: 7","Compile error"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A assumes character appending. C is an addition error. D is wrong because expression nesting is valid.","Operator precedence ensures the expression inside the parentheses (5 * 2) evaluates to 10 before text concatenation occurs.","A assumes character appending. C is an addition error. D is wrong because expression nesting is valid.","A assumes character appending. C is an addition error. D is wrong because expression nesting is valid."]},{"question_text":"What happens if a class inherits from two different classes in Java?","options":["The child class merges properties from both parents seamlessly.","The compiler rejects it because Java does not support multiple class inheritance.","The JVM resolves conflicts by using the first class mentioned.","The class compiles but throws a runtime linkage error."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A, C, and D are incorrect because the compiler blocks multiple class inheritance.","Java allows multiple inheritance only through interfaces to avoid diamond problem complexities.","A, C, and D are incorrect because the compiler blocks multiple class inheritance.","A, C, and D are incorrect because the compiler blocks multiple class inheritance."]},{"question_text":"Which component represents a critical system issue that applications should not try to catch?","options":["java.lang.Exception","java.lang.RuntimeException","java.lang.Error","java.lang.Throwable"],"correct_option_index":2,"option_explanations":["A and B represent standard catchable exception trees. D is the root class of both exceptions and errors.","A and B represent standard catchable exception trees. D is the root class of both exceptions and errors.","Classes extending Error (like OutOfMemoryError) signal severe system failures that applications should rarely attempt to catch.","A and B represent standard catchable exception trees. D is the root class of both exceptions and errors."]},{"question_text":"What will be the output of the following code snippet?\n\n```java\npublic class PostIncrement {\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        int i = 0;\r\n        i = i++;\r\n        System.out.println(i);\r\n    }\r\n}\n```","options":["1","0","-1","Compile error"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A assumes the incremented value is retained. C and D are incorrect results for this standard execution loop.","The post-increment operator i++ copies the original value (0) to a temporary buffer, increments i to 1, and then overwrites i with the buffered value (0).","A assumes the incremented value is retained. C and D are incorrect results for this standard execution loop.","A assumes the incremented value is retained. C and D are incorrect results for this standard execution loop."]},{"question_text":"Which of the following is an accurate description of composition?","options":["An \"Is-A\" structural inheritance architecture pattern.","A \"Has-A\" relationship achieved by embedding object handles.","A technique used to catch multiple checked exceptions.","A mechanism to compile source files into bytecodes."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A defines inheritance. C describes multi-catch exception blocks. D describes the compilation process.","Composition builds complex classes by nesting other objects as instance fields, modeling a \"Has-A\" relationship.","A defines inheritance. C describes multi-catch exception blocks. D describes the compilation process.","A defines inheritance. C describes multi-catch exception blocks. D describes the compilation process."]},{"question_text":"What is the default access modifier for a class member if no modifier is explicitly specified?","options":["public","private","protected","package-private (default)"],"correct_option_index":3,"option_explanations":["A, B, and C require explicit keywords to apply their respective visibility scopes.","A, B, and C require explicit keywords to apply their respective visibility scopes.","A, B, and C require explicit keywords to apply their respective visibility scopes.","Omitting an access modifier defaults visibility to package-private, restricting access to classes within the same package directory."]},{"question_text":"What happens when a method is declared static?","options":["It cannot accept any primitive parameters.","It belongs to the class itself rather than object instances.","It forces the method to run asynchronously.","It must be overridden by every subclass definition."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A is incorrect since parameters work normally. C describes concurrent tasks. D is incorrect because static methods cannot be overridden.","Static methods are bound to the class scope and can be invoked directly without instantiating an object.","A is incorrect since parameters work normally. C describes concurrent tasks. D is incorrect because static methods cannot be overridden.","A is incorrect since parameters work normally. C describes concurrent tasks. D is incorrect because static methods cannot be overridden."]},{"question_text":"Consider the following code block. What will be the output?\n\n```java\npublic class FinallyReturn {\r\n    public static int getValue() {\r\n        try { return 1; }\r\n        finally { return 2; }\r\n    }\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        System.out.println(getValue());\r\n    }\r\n}\n```","options":["1","2","3","Compile error"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A is bypassed by the finally block''s return statement. C and D are invalid interpretations of this valid control structure.","A return statement in the finally block overrides any previous return statement or pending exception in the try or catch blocks.","A is bypassed by the finally block''s return statement. C and D are invalid interpretations of this valid control structure.","A is bypassed by the finally block''s return statement. C and D are invalid interpretations of this valid control structure."]},{"question_text":"Which keyword must be included in a method header signature to signal that it may propagate checked exceptions?","options":["throw","throws","checked","transient"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A explicitly raises an exception instance. C is not a valid Java keyword. D handles serialization exclusion.","The throws keyword on a method signature alerts callers to potential checked exceptions that they must handle or declare.","A explicitly raises an exception instance. C is not a valid Java keyword. D handles serialization exclusion.","A explicitly raises an exception instance. C is not a valid Java keyword. D handles serialization exclusion."]},{"question_text":"What is the operational trait of a LinkedHashMap compared to a standard HashMap?","options":["It sorts elements in natural dictionary order.","It maintains elements in their exact insertion order.","It consumes significantly less heap memory space.","It completely blocks the usage of null pointer entries."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A describes sorted structures. C is incorrect because tracking nodes increases memory overhead. D allows nulls normally.","LinkedHashMap maintains a doubly linked list through its entries, preserving insertion order during traversal.","A describes sorted structures. C is incorrect because tracking nodes increases memory overhead. D allows nulls normally.","A describes sorted structures. C is incorrect because tracking nodes increases memory overhead. D allows nulls normally."]},{"question_text":"What will be the output of the following code snippet?\n\n```java\npublic class ArrayAssign {\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        int[] arr1 = {1, 2, 3};\r\n        int[] arr2 = arr1;\r\n        arr2[0] = 9;\r\n        System.out.println(arr1[0]);\r\n    }\r\n}\n```","options":["1","9","0","NullPointerException"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A assumes a deep copy was performed. C and D are incorrect since both handles point to a valid, shared array allocation.","Array variables store reference handles. Assigning arr1 to arr2 copies the reference, meaning both point to the same array object in memory.","A assumes a deep copy was performed. C and D are incorrect since both handles point to a valid, shared array allocation.","A assumes a deep copy was performed. C and D are incorrect since both handles point to a valid, shared array allocation."]},{"question_text":"Which mechanism allows a single catch block to intercept multiple unrelated exception types?","options":["Cascading multiple distinct try catch blocks.","Separating exception types with a pipe symbol (|) in a single catch.","Nesting catch statement arguments inside an array array.","Converting exceptions into standard primitive types."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A uses verbose syntax. C and D introduce invalid type management concepts.","Introduced in Java 7, the multi-catch feature uses a vertical pipe symbol (|) to handle multiple distinct exception types in a single block.","A uses verbose syntax. C and D introduce invalid type management concepts.","A uses verbose syntax. C and D introduce invalid type management concepts."]},{"question_text":"What restriction applies to variables declared inside an interface block?","options":["They must use private visibility access tags.","They are implicitly public, static, and final.","They must be re-initialized by implementing classes.","They are excluded from modern runtime class setups."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A violates visibility rules. C is incorrect because final fields cannot be reassigned. D is structurally false.","Interface fields serve as constants and are implicitly defined as public static final, requiring an immediate value assignment.","A violates visibility rules. C is incorrect because final fields cannot be reassigned. D is structurally false.","A violates visibility rules. C is incorrect because final fields cannot be reassigned. D is structurally false."]},{"question_text":"What will be the output of the following code snippet?\n\n```java\npublic class StringEquals {\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        String s1 = \"test\";\r\n        Object s2 = new String(\"test\");\r\n        System.out.println(s1.equals(s2));\r\n    }\r\n}\n```","options":["true","false","Compile-time type error","Runtime Exception"],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["String.equals() accepts any Object as an argument. It performs an internal type check and compares character values, evaluating to true.","B assumes reference checking instead of value validation. C and D are wrong because the method signature accepts Object.","B assumes reference checking instead of value validation. C and D are wrong because the method signature accepts Object.","B assumes reference checking instead of value validation. C and D are wrong because the method signature accepts Object."]},{"question_text":"Which of the following is true about a final class?","options":["It cannot be instantiated using the new keyword.","It cannot be extended by any other class.","All methods inside it must be marked static.","It can only contain abstract method signatures."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A describes abstract class behavior. C and D mistake final rules for utility or abstract design constraints.","Marking a class as final prevents it from being extended, ensuring its implementation cannot be modified by subclasses.","A describes abstract class behavior. C and D mistake final rules for utility or abstract design constraints.","A describes abstract class behavior. C and D mistake final rules for utility or abstract design constraints."]},{"question_text":"What is the consequence of declaring a method parameter as final?","options":["The method parameter cannot be read inside the method.","The method parameter value cannot be modified inside the method.","The method must return a final object type reference.","The parameter is automatically converted into a static global."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A blocks functional access. C and D apply incorrect structural scopes or return rules.","A final parameter acts as a read-only variable within the method body, preventing any reassignment of that parameter.","A blocks functional access. C and D apply incorrect structural scopes or return rules.","A blocks functional access. C and D apply incorrect structural scopes or return rules."]},{"question_text":"Consider the following code. What will be the output?\n\n```java\npublic class FieldOverride {\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        Parent classObj = new Child();\r\n        System.out.println(classObj.msg);\r\n    }\r\n}\r\nclass Parent { String msg = \"Parent\"; }\r\nclass Child extends Parent { String msg = \"Child\"; }\n```","options":["Parent","Child","Null","Compile error"],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["Fields are resolved statically at compile time based on the reference type (Parent), not the actual runtime object type.","B assumes fields participate in runtime polymorphism. C and D are incorrect because variable shadowing is legal.","B assumes fields participate in runtime polymorphism. C and D are incorrect because variable shadowing is legal.","B assumes fields participate in runtime polymorphism. C and D are incorrect because variable shadowing is legal."]},{"question_text":"Which type of exception is ArithmeticException?","options":["Checked exception","Unchecked exception","Compile-time error condition","Fatal unrecoverable system error"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A requires explicit compile-time management. C and D confuse runtime exceptions with syntax or critical system failures.","ArithmeticException inherits from RuntimeException, making it an unchecked exception that doesn''t require explicit handling.","A requires explicit compile-time management. C and D confuse runtime exceptions with syntax or critical system failures.","A requires explicit compile-time management. C and D confuse runtime exceptions with syntax or critical system failures."]},{"question_text":"What structural pattern is required to call a sibling constructor within the same class?","options":["super()","this()","current()","invoke()"],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A invokes a parent class constructor. C and D are not valid keywords or syntax for constructor chaining.","The this() expression handles constructor chaining within the same class and must be the first statement in the constructor body.","A invokes a parent class constructor. C and D are not valid keywords or syntax for constructor chaining.","A invokes a parent class constructor. C and D are not valid keywords or syntax for constructor chaining."]},{"question_text":"What happens if you catch an exception type but then rethrow it inside the same catch block?","options":["The application immediately crashes with a compiler syntax error.","The exception continues propagating up the execution call stack.","The finally block is bypassed to let the exception exit.","The catch block enters an infinite execution loop."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A is valid syntax. C is incorrect because finally always executes before propagation. D is structurally false.","Rethrowing an exception passes it out of the current method scope to look for a matching handler further up the call stack.","A is valid syntax. C is incorrect because finally always executes before propagation. D is structurally false.","A is valid syntax. C is incorrect because finally always executes before propagation. D is structurally false."]},{"question_text":"What will be the output of the following code snippet?\n\n```java\npublic class CompareTest {\r\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\r\n        String s1 = \"a\";\r\n        String s2 = \"b\";\r\n        System.out.println(s1.compareTo(s2) < 0);\r\n    }\r\n}\n```","options":["true","false","Compile error","Runtime Exception"],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["String.compareTo compares strings lexicographically. Since \"a\" comes before \"b\", it returns a negative value, evaluating to true.","B reverses dictionary order logic. C and D are incorrect since alphabetical comparison is a standard operation.","B reverses dictionary order logic. C and D are incorrect since alphabetical comparison is a standard operation.","B reverses dictionary order logic. C and D are incorrect since alphabetical comparison is a standard operation."]},{"question_text":"Which of the following is true about a protected constructor?","options":["It can only be invoked by classes in the same package or subclasses.","It blocks all instance allocation attempts across the application.","It forces the class to be declared as an abstract structure.","It can be accessed globally by any external class package."],"correct_option_index":0,"option_explanations":["A protected constructor restricts instantiation access to classes within the same package or inheriting subclasses.","B describes private constructor setups. C and D misstate the visibility rules of the protected access modifier.","B describes private constructor setups. C and D misstate the visibility rules of the protected access modifier.","B describes private constructor setups. C and D misstate the visibility rules of the protected access modifier."]},{"question_text":"What happens if a try block executes completely without throwing any exceptions?","options":["The catch blocks run as a fallback validation step.","The catch blocks are skipped, and the finally block executes normally.","The application halts immediately to wait for human input.","The JVM resets all internal class-level variables to null."],"correct_option_index":1,"option_explanations":["A violates normal flow control. C and D describe incorrect runtime actions.","When a try block completes successfully, all associated catch blocks are bypassed, and execution moves directly to the finally block.","A violates normal flow control. C and D describe incorrect runtime actions.","A violates normal flow control. C and D describe incorrect runtime actions."]}]'::jsonb,
